            "remediation": {
                "description": "<ol>\n\t<li>Sign in to AWS Management Console.</li>\n\t<li>Navigate to Amazon RDS dashboard at <a href=\"https://console.aws.amazon.com/rds/\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://console.aws.amazon.com/rds/</a>([https://console.aws.amazon.com/rds/)|https://console.aws.amazon.com/rds/)].</li>\n\t<li>In the left navigation panel, under <b>Amazon RDS, click Snapshots</b>.</li>\n\t<li>Select a snapshot tab from the snapshot category tabs to list all related RDS snapshots owned by your AWS account.</li>\n\t<li>Select the unencrypted RDS snapshot that you want to encrypt (see Audit section part I to identify the right resource).</li>\n\t<li>Click the <b>Actions dropdown button from the dashboard top menu and select Copy Snapshot</b>.</li>\n\t<li>On the *<b>Copy Snapshot</b>* page, perform the following commands:\n\t<ol>\n\t\t<li>From the <b>Destination Region</b> dropdown list, select the region where you want to write the copy of the selected snapshot.</li>\n\t\t<li>In the <b>New DB Snapshot Identifier</b> box, type a unique name/identifier for the new RDS snapshot (copy).</li>\n\t\t<li>(Optional) From <b>Target Option Group</b> dropdown list, select an option group to associate with your target database snapshot.</li>\n\t\t<li>(Optional) Select <b>Copy Tags</b> checkbox if you want your new snapshot to have the same tags as the source snapshot.</li>\n\t\t<li>In the <b>Encryption</b> section, select <b>Enable encryption</b> to turn on encryption at rest for the new RDS snapshot. Select <b>(default) aws/rds</b> from the *Master Key*dropdown list to use the default master key (also known as AWS Managed Key), a predefined key that protects your RDS database snapshot when no other key is defined for this purpose.</li>\n\t\t<li>Click <b>Copy Snapshot</b> to confirm the action. The process will take a couple of minutes to complete. Once created, you should see the encrypted RDS snapshot (copy) with the <b>Status</b> set to <b>available</b>, listed on the <b>Snapshots</b> page.</li>\n\t</ol>\n\t</li>\n\t<li>Now that your Amazon RDS database snapshot is encrypted, you can safely delete the source (unencrypted) snapshot. </li>\n</ol>",
