            "remediation": {
                "description": "<ol>\n\t<li>Sign in to the AWS Management Console and open the Amazon S3 console at <a href=\"https://console.aws.amazon.com/s3/\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://console.aws.amazon.com/s3/</a>.</li>\n\t<li>Click on the Create bucket button from the dashboard top menu to launch a new S3 bucket.</li>\n\t<li>Within Create bucket setup window, perform the following actions:<br/>\nA. Inside the Bucket name box, provide a DNS-compliant bucket name that that doesn't contain periods.<br/>\nB. Select the appropriate AWS region from the Region dropdown list.<br/>\nC. Select the source bucket (i.e. the S3 bucket with non–DNS compliant name) from the Copy settings from an existing bucket dropdown list.<br/>\nD. Click Create to set up the new DNS-compliant S3 bucket.</li>\n\t<li>Now you can copy everything from the source bucket to the newly created S3 bucket.</li>\n\t<li>Once all your objects are copied from the source bucket to the DNS-compliant bucket, it is safe to remove the source bucket in order to stop incurring charges for it. To delete the required S3 bucket, perform the following:<br/>\nA. Select the bucket that you want to remove from your AWS account.<br/>\nB. Click Delete bucket from the S3 dashboard top menu.<br/>\nC. Inside Delete bucket confirmation box, enter the name of the bucket within Type the name of the bucket to confirm box, then click Confirm to remove the bucket.</li>\n\t<li>Repeat steps no. 3 – 6 to re-create other AWS S3 buckets with non–DNS compliant names, available in your AWS account.</li>\n</ol>",
